{
  "image_name": "b443d5d35648d283566d703113bc8b5b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares several gate-induced doping modes in a sample controlled through an electrolyte. In the unbiased case, positive and negative ions are randomly distributed in the electrolyte above the sample, with no obvious charge accumulation at the interfaces. When a gate voltage is applied in the electrostatic-doping configuration, the ions separate into electric double layers: negative ions gather near the top electrode, while positive ions accumulate near the sample surface, inducing an opposing electronic charge in the sample confined to a thin interfacial region. This emphasizes surface-only carrier modulation.\n\nA stronger regime, labeled electrochemical doping, shows a different behavior: ions and compensating electronic charges extend into the sample rather than remaining only at the interface, indicating bulk-like doping through ionic penetration or redox processes. The charge distribution is therefore thicker and more volumetric than in the purely electrostatic case.\n\nThe final schematic combines an electrolyte top gate with a solid back gate. This dual-gating setup appears to create opposite charge accumulations from the two sides of the sample, allowing independent control of top and bottom carrier populations and a more complex internal charge profile. Overall, the figure highlights the transition from neutral, to interfacial electrostatic charging, to deeper electrochemical doping, and then to bidirectional control with dual gating.",
